1
00:00:00,688 --> 00:00:01,521
(upbeat rock music)

2
00:00:01,521 --> 00:00:04,149
♪ In the not too distant future ♪

3
00:00:04,149 --> 00:00:07,368
♪ Way down in Deep 13 ♪

4
00:00:07,368 --> 00:00:10,448
♪ Dr. Forrester and TV's Frank ♪

5
00:00:10,448 --> 00:00:13,768
♪ Were hatching an evil scheme ♪

6
00:00:13,768 --> 00:00:16,749
♪ They hired a temp by the name of Mike ♪

7
00:00:16,749 --> 00:00:19,800
♪ Just a regular Joe they didn't like ♪

8
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:22,778
♪ Their experiment
needed a good test case ♪

9
00:00:22,778 --> 00:00:24,448
♪ So they conked him on the noggin ♪

10
00:00:24,448 --> 00:00:26,600
♪ And they shot him into space ♪

11
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:28,183
- [Mike] Let me go.

12
00:00:29,338 --> 00:00:32,378
♪ We'll send him cheesy movies ♪

13
00:00:32,378 --> 00:00:35,448
♪ The worst we can find ♪

14
00:00:35,448 --> 00:00:38,549
♪ He'll have to sit and watch them all ♪

15
00:00:38,549 --> 00:00:41,778
♪ And we'll monitor his mind ♪

16
00:00:41,778 --> 00:00:44,480
♪ Now keep in mind Mike can't control ♪

17
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:48,000
♪ Where the movies begin or end ♪

18
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,008
♪ He'll try to keep his sanity ♪

19
00:00:51,008 --> 00:00:56,008
♪ With the help of his robot friends ♪

20
00:00:56,189 --> 00:00:57,591
♪ Robot roll call ♪

21
00:00:57,591 --> 00:01:02,591
♪ Cambot, Gypsy, Tom, Servo, Crow ♪

22
00:01:03,612 --> 00:01:06,722
♪ If you're wondering how
he eats and breathes ♪

23
00:01:06,722 --> 00:01:09,640
♪ And other science facts ♪

24
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:12,583
♪ Just repeat to yourself
it's just a show ♪

25
00:01:12,583 --> 00:01:15,994
♪ I should really just relax ♪

26
00:01:15,994 --> 00:01:20,411
♪ For "Mystery Science Theater 3000" ♪

27
00:01:23,151 --> 00:01:25,092
(metal door clanking)

28
00:01:25,092 --> 00:01:27,098
(space station beeping)
(door whooshing)

29
00:01:27,098 --> 00:01:32,098
(door zipping)
(door clanking)

30
00:01:34,457 --> 00:01:36,180
- See, I don't know what my area code is

31
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:38,614
'cause I'm up in the Milky
Way for crying out loud.

32
00:01:38,614 --> 00:01:40,670
(robots giggling)

33
00:01:40,670 --> 00:01:44,340
- Oh, hi everyone, Tom Servo
here on the Satellite of Love.

34
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:45,660
And this is my pal Crow.

35
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:48,070
- Yeah, and that goofy
looking guy back there?

36
00:01:48,070 --> 00:01:49,400
That's Mike Nelson.

37
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:51,170
He's trying to call his
grandma on the phone.

38
00:01:51,170 --> 00:01:53,060
It's a sweet gesture, isn't it?

39
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:54,930
But we explained to him
it's not that easy to do

40
00:01:54,930 --> 00:01:56,210
when you're stranded in space.

41
00:01:56,210 --> 00:01:57,380
- Yeah well you guys are never armed

42
00:01:57,380 --> 00:01:58,660
with a stack of calling cards

43
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:00,080
and a few little tricks like I am.

44
00:02:00,080 --> 00:02:01,574
Hello?
- Wow!

45
00:02:01,574 --> 00:02:02,830
- Hey, get around here, this is it!

46
00:02:02,830 --> 00:02:03,840
- What, you're kidding!
(telephone ringing)

47
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:05,616
You actually got your
grandma on the phone?

48
00:02:05,616 --> 00:02:06,449
- This is my grandma.

49
00:02:06,449 --> 00:02:08,930
(phone ringing)

50
00:02:08,930 --> 00:02:10,050
- Well Grandma's a mall walker,

51
00:02:10,050 --> 00:02:11,370
she's probably just getting in right now.

52
00:02:11,370 --> 00:02:13,020
- Right yeah.

53
00:02:13,020 --> 00:02:14,170
- [Grandma] Hello?

54
00:02:14,170 --> 00:02:16,890
- Hello, Grandma, this is
Mike, I'm stranded in space,

55
00:02:16,890 --> 00:02:19,130
you gotta help, you gotta
get someone to help me down!

56
00:02:19,130 --> 00:02:20,505
- [Grandma] Mike, what's that?

57
00:02:20,505 --> 00:02:21,338
Speak up.

58
00:02:21,338 --> 00:02:23,170
- I say, I've been
shanghaied on a satellite,

59
00:02:23,170 --> 00:02:25,260
you gotta get someone to
help me get outta here!

60
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:27,030
- [Grandma] I'm just
kidding, this is Katherine

61
00:02:27,030 --> 00:02:28,177
and I'm not in right now.

62
00:02:28,177 --> 00:02:30,760
I might be sitting for Tommy's kids

63
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:32,320
or else I'm at Linda's,

64
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:34,530
leave a message.
(answering machine beeping)

65
00:02:34,530 --> 00:02:36,923
- Grandma, listen very carefully,
this is Mike, I've been-

66
00:02:36,923 --> 00:02:38,146
(answering machine beeping)
(dial tone buzzing)

67
00:02:38,146 --> 00:02:40,813
(Mike groaning)

68
00:02:41,660 --> 00:02:43,420
That was my only chance.

69
00:02:43,420 --> 00:02:44,920
- Wow that's harsh, huh?

70
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:48,273
- That's okay, Mike,
we're your family now.

71
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:50,133
- Yeah.

72
00:02:51,609 --> 00:02:53,941
(telephone buttons beeping)

73
00:02:53,941 --> 00:02:56,941
(upbeat rock music)

74
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:00,850
- Sprint?
- No.

75
00:03:00,850 --> 00:03:02,713
- Mike, Mike, Mike, Mike.

76
00:03:02,713 --> 00:03:05,800
Oh, you should really make a note of that.

77
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:08,110
That you got a machine
so that if the bill comes

78
00:03:08,110 --> 00:03:10,110
and we're erroneously
charged for the call,

79
00:03:10,110 --> 00:03:11,870
then you have a paperwork
to back up your claim

80
00:03:11,870 --> 00:03:13,460
that we were charged for the call in error

81
00:03:13,460 --> 00:03:15,773
so that you won't have to
pay for the call, Mike.

82
00:03:16,699 --> 00:03:19,210
- But Crow, you lived
during the Great Depression,

83
00:03:19,210 --> 00:03:20,770
didn't you?

84
00:03:20,770 --> 00:03:22,900
- Oh Backrack and David are calling.

85
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:23,733
- They are?

86
00:03:23,733 --> 00:03:25,113
- Just push the button.

87
00:03:26,250 --> 00:03:28,330
- Laundry, laundry, oh!

88
00:03:28,330 --> 00:03:31,480
Mike, you would not
believe the day I've had,

89
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:33,860
I don't know how single parents do it!

90
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:36,890
Oh I was excited with the
arrival of young Frank but-

91
00:03:36,890 --> 00:03:39,220
- Dr. Raff, come on!

92
00:03:39,220 --> 00:03:41,640
- Clayton's on the phone right now, Frank.

93
00:03:41,640 --> 00:03:44,440
It's a 25 hour a day job

94
00:03:44,440 --> 00:03:45,690
keeping track of Frank.

95
00:03:45,690 --> 00:03:47,410
He's into everything.

96
00:03:47,410 --> 00:03:49,810
Sometimes I think we
both need a little break.

97
00:03:49,810 --> 00:03:51,960
That's why I've invented
the Frankenforcer.

98
00:03:52,994 --> 00:03:56,520
You see, the Frankenforcer
keeps little Frank suspended

99
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:58,740
in that disorienting no-man's-land

100
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:00,630
between floor and ceiling.

101
00:04:00,630 --> 00:04:03,470
The mat with the nails jutting out

102
00:04:03,470 --> 00:04:05,440
at a most dangerous angle,

103
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:07,410
gently discourages Frank

104
00:04:07,410 --> 00:04:11,309
from jumping out of the
Frankenforcer and into Dr. F's hair.

105
00:04:11,309 --> 00:04:14,160
- Wait!
- Just a little minute

106
00:04:14,160 --> 00:04:16,070
Frankie, there you go.

107
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:21,520
Now Dr. Forester has time
to tabulate and standardize

108
00:04:21,520 --> 00:04:24,940
results of our latest experiment,
postulate new theories

109
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:26,430
or just get the laundry done

110
00:04:26,430 --> 00:04:28,253
without worrying about this one.

111
00:04:29,190 --> 00:04:32,529
Plus, I've gotten to know
someone very special, me.

112
00:04:32,529 --> 00:04:34,086
(doctor chortling)

113
00:04:34,086 --> 00:04:36,690
Oh, gotta go Mike, we get fussy

114
00:04:36,690 --> 00:04:38,960
if we've been in there
more than a few weeks.

115
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:40,400
Immature little baby.

116
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:43,755
- I'm guessing Frank's
in the 90th percentile.

117
00:04:43,755 --> 00:04:44,588
(robots laugh)

118
00:04:44,588 --> 00:04:46,660
- Anyway, let me set the scene here.

119
00:04:46,660 --> 00:04:48,870
You're at your favorite
candlelit restaurant,

120
00:04:48,870 --> 00:04:52,100
spending time with someone very special.

121
00:04:52,100 --> 00:04:54,590
Exchanging lingering glances
over the feta cheese-

122
00:04:54,590 --> 00:04:55,423
- What?

123
00:04:55,423 --> 00:04:58,500
- Laughing low, throaty laughs
over a shared private joke,

124
00:04:58,500 --> 00:04:59,600
fingers entwined.

125
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:00,820
- Would you just get on with it, Servo?

126
00:05:00,820 --> 00:05:03,220
- And sometimes you just
need your server's attention!

127
00:05:03,220 --> 00:05:04,618
Enter the Waitervator.

128
00:05:04,618 --> 00:05:06,420
(robots laugh)

129
00:05:06,420 --> 00:05:08,870
- I'll be right over here, hi Tad!

130
00:05:08,870 --> 00:05:10,270
- Don't waste your valuable time

131
00:05:10,270 --> 00:05:11,800
trying to get your waiter's attention,

132
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:14,580
just let the Waitervator
do the dirty work!

133
00:05:14,580 --> 00:05:16,470
- I'll be with you in a moment please,

134
00:05:16,470 --> 00:05:17,584
I'm in the weeds here.

135
00:05:17,584 --> 00:05:20,251
(Crow mumbling)

136
00:05:22,780 --> 00:05:26,077
- Okay, well what do you think, sirs?

137
00:05:27,170 --> 00:05:29,500
- I think you'll be having our special.

138
00:05:29,500 --> 00:05:34,397
A tough stringy entree
called Teenage Strangler.

139
00:05:34,397 --> 00:05:35,370
(grumbling) Frank!

140
00:05:35,370 --> 00:05:39,040
Served with a distasteful
little appetizer about love

141
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:40,161
or some such slop.

142
00:05:40,161 --> 00:05:41,480
Frank.

143
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:43,917
I think I'm going to have
to put Frank down for a nap.

144
00:05:43,917 --> 00:05:44,887
Permanently.

145
00:05:44,887 --> 00:05:45,970
(Dr. Forrester grumbles)

146
00:05:45,970 --> 00:05:46,803
Frank.

147
00:05:47,730 --> 00:05:49,950
- No, no, you had three big
bites of my lettuce-wich.

148
00:05:49,950 --> 00:05:51,420
- Yeah, but you had my
cling-peach, so it evened it out.

149
00:05:51,420 --> 00:05:52,945
- I only had a little
taste of your cling-peach,

150
00:05:52,945 --> 00:05:54,246
you don't expect me
- You eat the whole half.

151
00:05:54,246 --> 00:05:55,529
- I'm not going to pay
for your cling-peach.

152
00:05:55,529 --> 00:05:56,700
- I'm not going to have to pay for it.

153
00:05:56,700 --> 00:06:00,298
- Ladies, ladies, we have movie time.

154
00:06:00,298 --> 00:06:02,490
(whoosh)

155
00:06:02,490 --> 00:06:04,053
(robotic door opening)

156
00:06:04,053 --> 00:06:05,582
(clunk)

157
00:06:05,582 --> 00:06:06,515
(bubbles)

158
00:06:06,515 --> 00:06:07,580
(zipping)

159
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:08,999
(beeping)

160
00:06:08,999 --> 00:06:09,960
(metal door clank)

161
00:06:09,960 --> 00:06:11,609
(clanking)

162
00:06:11,609 --> 00:06:12,830
(old-silent-movie-music)

163
00:06:12,830 --> 00:06:15,290
- [Mike] If you two would just
get together and pay the tab.

164
00:06:15,290 --> 00:06:16,980
- [Servo] Is this love
or is it just rough sex

165
00:06:16,980 --> 00:06:18,177
with Michael Douglas?

166
00:06:18,177 --> 00:06:19,583
- [Crow] Wow. What?

167
00:06:22,080 --> 00:06:24,230
- [Mike] By Paul H.
Landis, who's never known

168
00:06:24,230 --> 00:06:25,280
the touch of a woman.

169
00:06:26,230 --> 00:06:29,563
(romantic 1920's music)

170
00:06:30,850 --> 00:06:32,540
- [Crow] Collaborator Lillian Bilkey,

171
00:06:32,540 --> 00:06:35,902
who's single and lives in a
furnished room with six cats.

172
00:06:35,902 --> 00:06:36,845
- [Mike] Lillian Bilkey.

173
00:06:36,845 --> 00:06:37,678
- [Robot 1] Bilkey.

174
00:06:37,678 --> 00:06:39,178
- [Robot 2] Bilkey

175
00:06:40,848 --> 00:06:45,031
- [Mike] Well, except for
Tom Hanks and Peter Scolari.

176
00:06:45,031 --> 00:06:47,459
(Servo gasping and gulping)

177
00:06:47,459 --> 00:06:48,410
- [Servo] Out of my way, I want to catch

178
00:06:48,410 --> 00:06:50,410
the end of Bobby Hackett's trumpet solo.

179
00:06:56,710 --> 00:06:59,710
- [Crow] Miss Channing, there's
an Eve Herington to see you.

180
00:07:02,091 --> 00:07:04,291
- [Mike] t's like Van
Goghs's room in Arles.

181
00:07:06,030 --> 00:07:08,790
- [Servo] So, um, where's Waldo?

182
00:07:08,790 --> 00:07:10,390
- [Mike] Ah, he's under the bed.

183
00:07:13,673 --> 00:07:15,689
- [Crow] Geez, how many
time was she held back?

184
00:07:15,689 --> 00:07:17,710
(Servo chuckles)

185
00:07:17,710 --> 00:07:18,600
- Hi, Peg.

186
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:19,433
- [Mike] Hi, Mom.

187
00:07:19,433 --> 00:07:20,300
- God, you look more excited

188
00:07:20,300 --> 00:07:21,460
than I've ever seen you before

189
00:07:21,460 --> 00:07:22,484
and that's say something.

190
00:07:22,484 --> 00:07:23,923
(Crow meows and hisses)

191
00:07:23,923 --> 00:07:24,756
- You'll never guess.

192
00:07:24,756 --> 00:07:25,810
I got engaged to Joe.

193
00:07:25,810 --> 00:07:27,470
We're going to be married
at the end of the semester.

194
00:07:27,470 --> 00:07:28,780
Right after Joe graduates.

195
00:07:28,780 --> 00:07:29,613
- What?

196
00:07:29,613 --> 00:07:30,580
You've only been going steady

197
00:07:30,580 --> 00:07:32,400
with him for a couple of months.

198
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:34,720
- Exactly three months and three days.

199
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:36,067
- [Servo] Love has made me anal.

200
00:07:36,067 --> 00:07:36,900
(Servo laughs)

201
00:07:36,900 --> 00:07:39,210
- But, Peg, are you going
to drop out of college

202
00:07:39,210 --> 00:07:40,369
before you graduate?

203
00:07:40,369 --> 00:07:42,980
I mean, you've only
got another year to go.

204
00:07:42,980 --> 00:07:45,650
- Oh, Liz, everyone isn't
as slow as you and Andy.

205
00:07:45,650 --> 00:07:47,810
- [Crow] Besides you're a Romulan.

206
00:07:47,810 --> 00:07:49,220
- I want to get married in June.

207
00:07:49,220 --> 00:07:50,130
- [Mike] Mm, get the cops.

208
00:07:50,130 --> 00:07:51,400
- Joe has been told he's got a good chance

209
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:53,610
of getting a job at a
pro-football team this summer.

210
00:07:53,610 --> 00:07:54,730
- As a water boy.

211
00:07:54,730 --> 00:07:55,563
- He'll still would make that.

212
00:07:55,563 --> 00:07:56,580
- Are you going to phone your mom and dad

213
00:07:56,580 --> 00:07:57,700
about getting engaged.

214
00:07:57,700 --> 00:07:58,970
- [Crow] Actually they're married already.

215
00:07:58,970 --> 00:07:59,803
Oh, oh I see.

216
00:07:59,803 --> 00:08:02,320
- I'm scared stiff about
what mother will say.

217
00:08:02,320 --> 00:08:03,420
She'll raise the roof.

218
00:08:04,750 --> 00:08:07,010
I think I'll send a telegram instead.

219
00:08:07,010 --> 00:08:09,170
- Don't be silly, Peg.

220
00:08:09,170 --> 00:08:11,670
You always talk as though your
mother was an ogre or some-

221
00:08:11,670 --> 00:08:13,970
- [Mike] Well, I've known
her for fifty years.

222
00:08:15,283 --> 00:08:16,593
- Mother will be furious.

223
00:08:17,690 --> 00:08:20,620
Every so often she writes to
ask if I've seen Pete Standard.

224
00:08:20,620 --> 00:08:21,870
You know, just because his father's

225
00:08:21,870 --> 00:08:23,220
a big time real estate man

226
00:08:23,220 --> 00:08:25,540
and he's mother's idea of perfect catch.

227
00:08:25,540 --> 00:08:28,140
- He's just go the the Glen-Gery account.

228
00:08:28,140 --> 00:08:31,363
- Compared to Joe, Pete's so dull.

229
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:34,030
Joe's so handsome.

230
00:08:34,030 --> 00:08:36,016
- [Crow] Handsome equals not dull.

231
00:08:36,016 --> 00:08:36,849
- [Servo] Ding.

232
00:08:36,849 --> 00:08:38,590
- Honestly, Peg, I don't
know how many times

233
00:08:38,590 --> 00:08:40,290
ever since we were in high school together

234
00:08:40,290 --> 00:08:42,950
I've heard you go on the same
way about some man or other.

235
00:08:42,950 --> 00:08:45,010
- Oh but this is different,
Liz, really it is.

236
00:08:45,010 --> 00:08:47,100
- [Mike] He's anatomically
correct and everything.

237
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:48,820
- It's the real thing.

238
00:08:48,820 --> 00:08:50,570
- I remember when I first
felt that way about Andy.

239
00:08:50,570 --> 00:08:52,420
- [Servo] At the turn of the century.

240
00:08:52,420 --> 00:08:54,087
- I think about him all the time.

241
00:08:54,087 --> 00:08:57,750
Gosh, Peggy, you just
can't rush into marriage.

242
00:08:57,750 --> 00:08:58,583
- [Crow] Why not?

243
00:08:58,583 --> 00:08:59,416
Julian Roberts did.

244
00:08:59,416 --> 00:09:00,249
- Mm.

245
00:09:02,360 --> 00:09:03,490
- Don't mind my saying this,

246
00:09:03,490 --> 00:09:08,000
but you and Andy seem so, so-

247
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:09,980
- So old fashioned I suppose you mean?

248
00:09:09,980 --> 00:09:11,090
- [Mike] No, just old.

249
00:09:11,090 --> 00:09:14,230
- Well you've been going
together for a year and half.

250
00:09:14,230 --> 00:09:16,283
Last month, you finally became engaged.

251
00:09:17,160 --> 00:09:18,157
I'm just not like that.

252
00:09:18,157 --> 00:09:19,360
- [Servo] I'm easy.

253
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:20,193
- Well that's fine,

254
00:09:20,193 --> 00:09:22,380
but are you sure you'll
always been in love

255
00:09:22,380 --> 00:09:23,440
like you are right now?

256
00:09:23,440 --> 00:09:26,090
